--
instantiating 'std::__enable_if_t' with <FALSE
--
KNOWNBUG (morphed): the originally-documented hard error -- concretizing
unique_ptr's =delete'd deleter constructor template into
enable_if_t<false>::type during class instantiation ([temp.inst]/2) -- is
FIXED by the template-machinery work (the disallowed pattern above guards
against regression).  The remaining failure is a value bug with a single root:
unique_ptr's default/nullptr *constructor template* specialization is created
with a nil body (never sourced from the member template of the lazily
completed instance), so construction is a no-op and the stored pointer stays
nondet; release/reset/move-assignment and delete preconditions all fail
downstream.  See cpp11_unique_ptr_default_ctor_null for the minimal form.
Same deferred member-body instantiation gap as the map/tuple cases.  Flip to
CORE once member bodies are instantiated on odr-use.
